{
  /* The relay's own type-check program, and the reason it is a *separate* file rather than a
     wider `include` in `tsconfig.json` is the same fence `.storybook/tsconfig.json` exists
     for, pointed at a different runtime.

     `relay/` runs in workerd. It needs `@cloudflare/workers-types`, which declares `Request`,
     `Response`, `WebSocket`, `DurableObjectState` and about four hundred other globals — a
     whole platform's worth of ambient declarations. The app under `src/` runs in a WebView2
     webview and must see the DOM's versions of those names and nothing else; the root
     `tsconfig.json` pins `"types": []` for exactly that reason and says so at length. Merging
     the two programs would put workerd's globals in front of the app's, which is a class of
     bug nobody would think to look for.

     Nothing had to be *excluded* to keep them apart: the root config's `include` is `["src"]`,
     which is `<repo>/src` and does not match `<repo>/relay/src`. This file's `include` is the
     mirror image. `npm run build` runs `tsc`, then `tsc -p .storybook`, then this one, so all
     three programs are checked by `npm run verify` and none of them can see the others'
     ambient types.

     `relay/`'s `*.test.ts` files are deliberately inside this program rather than outside it: a
     test that does not type-check is a test that can drift from the function it is asserting
     about. This read "`relay/src/log.test.ts` … is the only file in `relay/` that the vitest
     suite runs", which was true when `log.ts` was the only pure module here and is not now — the
     root vitest also runs `claim`, `entitlement`, `md5`, `patreon` and `token`'s tests. Rather
     than keep a count in a comment, take the rule: **every pure decision in `relay/` is tested by
     the root vitest, and every one of those tests type-checks here.** */
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    /* No `DOM`: this is not a browser. `@cloudflare/workers-types` supplies the fetch, stream
       and URL globals workerd actually has, which are a different and smaller set. */
    "lib": ["ES2022"],
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "types": ["@cloudflare/workers-types"],

    "strict": true,
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "noEmit": true
  },
  "include": ["relay/src"]
}
